{# =============================================================================
   SupaZip — CLI archive listing table (Tera template)
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Purpose   : Define the visual contract for the `supazip list` stdout table.
               The CLI today prints a hand-formatted `printf`-style table; this
               file freezes the column widths, alignment, rule character, and
               the entry-count trailer so the future renderer (a follow-up PR)
               can be reviewed against a stable spec.

   Audience  : Power users of the SupaZip CLI; reviewers of the CLI refactor
               PR; the design system (DESIGN.md §"CLI styling" links here).

   Scope     : Human-readable layout contract. The CLI does not execute Tera;
               `design/cli-table.json` is the machine-readable mirror
               (widths, alignment, rule character, CRYPT literals, footer)
               included by `supazip-cli`. A unit test fails if this comment
               block and the JSON spec drift.

   Layout    : Fixed-width monospace, total width = 72 columns.
               IDX        : width  4, right-aligned
               METHOD     : width 12, left-aligned, padded/truncated
               SIZE       : width 12, right-aligned, humanised (KiB/MiB/GiB)
               COMPRESSED : width 12, right-aligned, humanised
               CRYPT      : width  8, left-aligned,  "yes" or "-"
               NAME       : unbounded, takes the rest of the line
               Columns are separated by exactly two spaces ("  ").
               The rule line is the Unicode box-drawing character
               U+2500 BOX DRAWINGS LIGHT HORIZONTAL ("─") repeated 72 times.
               The final "N entries" line is plain text, no rule, no border.

   Renderers : The format directives above are directly expressible in:
                 * Tera / Jinja2 (this file)        — using width-15 pad filters
                 * Rust `format!` / `writeln!`      — `{idx:>4}`, `{method:<12}`
                 * Python f-strings                  — f"{idx:>4d}"
               Any future implementation MUST produce the same byte stream
               as the worked example at the bottom of this file.

   Tera notes: Tera does not ship Rust-style format specifiers, so the
               column padding is done with `| truncate(length=12, end="…")`
               and `| padding(width=12, align="right")` style filters
               expressed via the `width`/`align` arguments below. Adjust
               filter names to match the engine you wire up; the *widths*
               and *alignments* above are what matter for the contract.
   ============================================================================= #}
IDX    METHOD        SIZE         COMPRESSED   CRYPT    NAME
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{% for row in rows -%}
{{ row.idx | width:4 | align:"right" }}  {{ row.method | truncate:12 | align:"left" | width:12 }}  {{ row.size | width:12 | align:"right" }}  {{ row.compressed | width:12 | align:"right" }}  {{ row.crypt | width:8 | align:"left" }}  {{ row.name }}
{% endfor -%}
{{ rows | length }} entries

{# =============================================================================
   Worked example
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Input (`rows`) — the three entries from `cli_round_trip.rs::build_small_zip`,
   the same fixture the live CLI test `list_zip_via_cli` consumes. Compressed
   sizes below are illustrative (real deflate ratios vary); the *layout* is
   the contract.

   rows = [
     { idx: 0, method: "Deflate",   size: 15, compressed:  19, crypt: "-",   name: "hello.txt"      },
     { idx: 1, method: "Deflate",   size: 22, compressed:  26, crypt: "-",   name: "greet/hi.txt"   },
     { idx: 2, method: "Store",     size:  4, compressed:   4, crypt: "yes", name: "data.bin"       },
   ]

   Expected rendered output (every line is 72 columns wide except the NAME
   tail, which extends to the right as needed; the rule line is exactly 72
   "─" characters — copy-paste verified):

   IDX    METHOD        SIZE         COMPRESSED   CRYPT    NAME
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      0  Deflate              15             19  -        hello.txt
      1  Deflate              22             26  -        greet/hi.txt
      2  Store                 4              4  yes     data.bin

   3 entries

   Width audit (excluding the two-space gaps between columns):
     4 (IDX) + 12 (METHOD) + 12 (SIZE) + 12 (COMPRESSED) + 8 (CRYPT) = 48
     + 4 gaps × 2 spaces = 8
     = 56 columns for the fixed prefix; the rule line is 72 columns so the
       trailing NAME column has 72 − 56 = 16 columns of visual breathing
       room before the first file in the example. Long names flow past
       72 columns without wrapping (terminals soft-wrap as they see fit).

   METHOD overflow: a method name longer than 12 chars must be truncated
   to 11 visible chars + a single "…" (U+2026 HORIZONTAL ELLIPSIS). Example:
     "LZMA2:48kfast" → "LZMA2:48kfas…"

   CRYPT: only the literals "yes" and "-" are permitted. Renderers MUST NOT
   leak "true"/"false"/"1"/"0" — those are for the data model, not the UI.

   Final line: "N entries" where N is `{{ rows | length }}`. The line has no
   leading rule and no border. Singular form ("1 entry") is intentionally
   not used — the CLI's tone is terse and consistent.
   ============================================================================= #}
